// PROOF (was mis-filed as a FRONTIER): a handle handed back on a LATER branch arm
// and then dropped is auto-discharged. This file compiles, boots, and does not
// leak — and the emitted program is the evidence.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THIS FILE USED TO CLAIM, AND WHY IT WAS WRONG
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// It was carried from the 02b replay asserting that "only a tor's FIRST declared
// branch has its payload obligations tracked for discharge", that this program
// therefore leaks its allocation on `| refused`, and that a compiler fix was
// needed. All of that is false. Disproved 2026-08-06 by reading the artifact.
//
// The source below frees on `| ok grown` and on `| rejected r`. Compile it and
// grep the emitted Zig for `free_event.handler`: there are THREE calls, and the
// third sits inside the `| refused e |>` branch —
//
//     // >>> BRANCH: input.kz:105  | refused e |>
//     _ = koru_unikraft.koru_alloc.free_event.handler(.{ .block = e.block });
//
// — the exact arm the old note said leaked. The compiler inserted it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ACTUAL MECHANISM
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Obligations are keyed by BINDING NAME, not by AST-node identity
// (src/auto_discharge_inserter.zig:97-98, addBinding:158 — a StringHashMap on the
// name). Arm position cannot matter. What varies is whether auto-discharge can
// ELECT a disposer for the state:
//
//   * exactly one unattended disposer -> inserted silently at the terminator;
//   * zero or several                 -> KORU030, naming the binding.
//
// The control that settles it from the other side: give a state a second
// unattended disposer and drop the LATER arm's handle. You get
//
//     error[KORU030]: Resource 'later' <live!> has multiple discharge options:
//                     close, scrap. Discharge explicitly.
//
// naming `later` — the second arm's binding. The checker sees later arms fine.
//
// WHY THE ORIGINAL OBSERVATION LOOKED LIKE ARM POSITION. 02b's controls against
// shipped `unikraft/blk` were real: KORU030 fired for a `<configured!>` handle on
// arm 1 and stayed silent for a `<stuck!>` handle on arm 2, in one compile. But
// the discriminator was the DISPOSER. `<stuck!>`'s only consumer is
// `blk:abandon` — one unattended disposer, auto-inserted. `<configured!>`'s could
// not be elected, so it was reported. Two arms, two disposer shapes, one correct
// compiler. Four labelled controls were run and every one of them varied arm
// position while none varied the disposer set, which is how a real asymmetry
// produced a wrong mechanism.
//
// KEPT, rather than deleted, for two reasons: it is a live regression against the
// auto-discharge path on later arms, and it is the record of a mis-attribution
// that reached a README, a status report, and a commissioned bugfix before anyone
// read the emitted code. See
// `koru/concepts/frag-a-reproducible-failure-localises-the-symptom-not-the-defect.md`.
// The `unikraft` namespace is declared here, not inherited: koruc's built-in
// alias probes the sibling koru-libs CHECKOUT, so in a worktree this file would
// otherwise compile against main's lifts instead of the ones beside it.
~std/compiler:paths {
    unikraft: {{ ENTRY }}/../..
}

~import unikraft/alloc
~import std/io

~unikraft/alloc:default
| ready heap |> unikraft/alloc:take(heap, bytes: 64)
    | block scratch |> unikraft/alloc:write(block: scratch, bytes: "sixty-four bytes about to be asked to become eight mib")
        | ok filled |> unikraft/alloc:resize(block: filled, bytes: 8388608)
            | ok grown |> unikraft/alloc:free(block: grown)
            | refused e |> std/io:print.ln("resize refused: {{ e.reason:s }}")
        | rejected r |> unikraft/alloc:untouched(r.block): u |> unikraft/alloc:free(block: u)
    | exhausted e |> std/io:print.ln("take({{ e.bytes:d }}) failed: {{ e.reason:s }}")
| unavailable why |> std/io:print.ln("no allocator: {{ why:s }}")
